Bootstrap: docker
From: rosettacommons/foundry:0.2.0-slim

# RFdiffusion3 0.2.0 -- on a cu128 torch, so it runs on sm_120.
#
# REBUILD of rfdiffusion3_0.2.0 for Blackwell, 2026-11-03. That file is kept
# beside this one because it is what every RFdiffusion3 number this lab holds
# was produced with.
#
# ── THE CHANGE IS THE CUDA BUILD, NOT THE VERSION ─────────────────────────
#
# Upstream's slim image ships torch 2.7.1+cu126, and cu126 predates Blackwell.
# Measured in the base 2026-11-03, the arch list carries sm_50 through sm_90
# with NO `compute_*` entry, so there is no PTX for the driver to JIT forward
# and sm_120 is not a slow path, it is no path.
#
# PyTorch publishes 2.7.1 for cu128 as well, so this installs the SAME torch
# version from a different CUDA index:
#   torch==2.7.1+cu126  ->  torch==2.7.1+cu128
# That is the whole diff. It is worth saying plainly because every other
# sm_120 rebuild in this registry moves a version and therefore owes a
# chemistry re-check: this one does not move the version at all, so the
# python-level API foundry resolved against is byte-identical and the only
# thing that changed is which GPU architectures the kernels were compiled for.
#
# WHAT STILL HAS TO BE CHECKED, and it is asserted below. foundry pins
# cuequivariance CUDA-12 wheels alongside its torch. cu128 is still CUDA 12, so
# those wheels should keep loading, but "should" is how this registry gets
# surprised, so %post imports them and the console scripts rather than assuming.
#
# Still the `slim` variant, WITHOUT checkpoints: the 14.6 GB of weights belong
# on the share, where refreshing them does not mean rebuilding an 18 GB image.
#
# Build:  chpc-tool build rfdiffusion3

%labels
    Tool         rfdiffusion3
    Version      0.2.0
    Upstream     https://github.com/RosettaCommons/foundry
    UpstreamRef  rc-foundry==0.2.0, torch 2.7.1 moved from cu126 to cu128
    Licence      BSD-3-Clause
    Base         rosettacommons/foundry:0.2.0-slim
    GPUArch      includes sm_120 -- asserted at build time, see %post
    Rebuild      2026-11-03, torch 2.7.1+cu128 for sm_120
    Maintainer   Craik Lab, UCSF

%post
    set -eux

    # A C COMPILER, and it is not optional on Blackwell. Triton JIT-compiles
    # its own `cuda_utils` module at RUNTIME, the first time a kernel launches,
    # by shelling out to a C compiler -- and the foundry base ships none, nor
    # does the cu126 image. On an L40S that never fired, because the Triton
    # wheel carries prebuilt kernels for sm_89; on sm_120 it has none, so the
    # JIT path is the only path and the fold dies with
    #
    #   RuntimeError: Failed to find C compiler. Please specify via CC
    #   environment variable.
    #
    # MEASURED 2026-11-09: this killed a real rf3 fold on an RTX 5090 at 17 s,
    # through the consuming project's own adapter, while `chpc-tool test
    # rfdiffusion3` passed on the same device. The test exercises the DESIGN
    # path and the adapter exercises FOLDING, and only the latter reaches
    # Triton -- so the entry's own test cannot stand in for this.
    #
    # build-essential rather than gcc alone: Triton's build helper links
    # against libpython and wants the usual headers, and the delta is small
    # next to a 4.5 GB image.
    apt-get update
    DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        build-essential
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
    cc --version | head -1

    # What the base gave us, before we touch it. NOTE the call: this image
    # answers torch.cuda.get_arch_list() with [] -- an empty list, despite
    # torch.version.cuda being 12.6 -- while torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
    # answers correctly with no device present. The registry's own measurement
    # used the latter, and so does every assert below. Measured 2026-11-03.
    python -c "import torch; print('base torch:', torch.__version__, torch.version.cuda, torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags())"

    # THERE IS NO PIP IN THIS IMAGE, and no uv or conda either: foundry ships a
    # uv-created venv at /app/foundry/.venv and does not install the installer.
    # `pip install` in a %post here dies with `pip: not found`, exit 127.
    # ensurepip is present (25.0.1), so bootstrap it into that same venv rather
    # than reaching for a second python.
    python -m ensurepip --upgrade
    python -m pip --version

    # Same version, cu128 build. Deps come from the cu128 index too, which is
    # where the matching nvidia-*-cu12 runtime wheels live; taking torch alone
    # with --no-deps would leave it against cu126 libraries.
    python -m pip install --no-cache-dir \
        --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128 \
        "torch==2.7.1+cu128"

    # Assert PROPERTIES, not imports (tools/README.md). get_arch_list() reads
    # build metadata and needs no device, so this is checkable at build time.
    python - <<'PY'
import sys

import torch

arch = torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
print("python", sys.version.split()[0], "torch", torch.__version__, "cuda", torch.version.cuda)
print("arch flags:", arch)

assert torch.__version__.startswith("2.7.1"), (
    f"expected torch 2.7.1 so the API matches what foundry pinned, got {torch.__version__}"
)
assert "+cu128" in torch.__version__, (
    f"expected the cu128 build, got {torch.__version__}. The override did not take."
)
assert "sm_120" in (arch or ""), (
    f"no sm_120 in {arch!r}. This image exists ONLY to carry sm_120 kernels; "
    "without them it is rfdiffusion3_0.2.0 with a reinstalled torch."
)
PY

    # The thing a version-identical swap could still break: foundry's CUDA
    # extension wheels were built against the cu126 torch.
    python - <<'PY'
import importlib

for mod in ("cuequivariance", "cuequivariance_torch", "cuequivariance_ops_torch_cu12"):
    try:
        importlib.import_module(mod)
        print("import ok:", mod)
    except ImportError as exc:
        print("absent (not fatal if foundry does not use it):", mod, exc)
PY

    # Triton's runtime JIT must be able to find a compiler, asserted through
    # the EXACT lookup triton/runtime/build.py performs -- os.environ["CC"]
    # first, then shutil.which over a candidate list. Asserting `cc --version`
    # in the shell would pass on an image where triton still fails, because the
    # shell's PATH and the python process's are not required to agree.
    #
    # It stops at compiler discovery on purpose. The next step, CudaUtils(),
    # needs a live CUDA driver, which a login node or a build host does not
    # have -- so this is the most that is honestly checkable at build time, and
    # the fold itself is still what qualifies the image.
    python - <<'PY'
import os
import shutil

cc = os.environ.get("CC") or shutil.which("cc") or shutil.which("gcc") or shutil.which("clang")
assert cc, (
    "no C compiler visible to python. Triton JIT-compiles cuda_utils at the "
    "first kernel launch and dies with 'Failed to find C compiler'; on sm_120 "
    "there are no prebuilt kernels, so that path is not optional."
)
print("triton will find a C compiler at:", cc)

import triton
print("triton", triton.__version__)
PY

    # And the console scripts, which is what a design job actually invokes.
    for s in rfd3 rf3 mpnn foundry; do command -v "$s"; done
    python -c "import sys; assert sys.version_info[:2] == (3, 12), sys.version"

    { echo "base: rosettacommons/foundry:0.2.0-slim"; \
      echo "torch: 2.7.1+cu128 (reinstalled over cu126 for sm_120)"; \
      date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ; } > /opt/.built-from

%environment
    export LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    # FOUNDRY_CHECKPOINT_DIRS is deliberately NOT set here -- it names a
    # location on this site's share, which the registry owns. foundry's own
    # default is ~/.foundry/checkpoints, which would put 14.6 GB on a home quota.

%test
    set -eu
    python - <<'PY'
import torch
arch = torch._C._cuda_getArchFlags()
assert "sm_120" in (arch or ""), f"no sm_120 in {arch!r}"
print("torch", torch.__version__, "arch ok:", arch)
PY
    rfd3 --help > /dev/null
    foundry list-available > /dev/null

%runscript
    exec rfd3 "$@"

%help
    RFdiffusion3 0.2.0 on torch 2.7.1+cu128, so it runs on sm_120 (Blackwell).

      chpc-tool exec rfdiffusion3 -- rfd3 --help

    Identical to the rfdiffusion3_0.2.0 entry except that torch is the cu128
    build of the SAME 2.7.1 version, so no python API moved. Checkpoints are
    not in the image; point FOUNDRY_CHECKPOINT_DIRS at the share.
